namespace ScatoloneDownloader.Mtg
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Informal effect ontology applied to cube cards. A card can carry several
    /// effects at once (e.g. an ETB creature that also removes a permanent), so
    /// this is a <see cref="FlagsAttribute"/> bitset combined with <c>|</c>.
    ///
    /// Structural buckets Creature and Land are intentionally NOT effects — they
    /// are covered by <see cref="MacroType"/> and the view tree. This enum holds
    /// only functional effects. Members are authored via the tagging tool and
    /// persisted by name (not packed int) for readable git diffs.
    ///
    /// Deliberately absent, decided 2026-09-05 — every tag is a question asked of
    /// 30k cards, so one that never changes a design decision costs and returns
    /// nothing:
    ///   - FOGS. A Fog prevents a combat, it does not answer a threat. It buys a
    ///     turn rather than saving a permanent, which is why it is not folded into
    ///     <see cref="Protection"/> either, despite also being instant-speed
    ///     prevention.
    ///   - LIFEGAIN. A resource, not an interaction. Worth a tag only for a cube
    ///     with an archetype built on it; this one has none.
    /// </summary>
    [Flags]
    public enum CardEffect
    {
        None            = 0,
        Tokens          = 1 << 0,   // put CREATURE bodies onto the battlefield (Lingering Souls, Bitterblossom, Grave Titan) — and yours, not somebody else's. A Treasure, Clue, Food or Lander token is a resource the card hands over in passing, not a board presence, and Afterlife's Spirit goes to the creature's own controller; neither is what a go-wide deck is built on. Narrowed 2026-09-15, and WIDENED the same day in the other direction: a body need not arrive as a token at all, so earthbend and "all lands are 1/1 creatures" (Nature's Revolt) count — a go-wide deck cares about the creatures, not the wording that made them. Reaffirmed and widened 2026-09-19. Earthbend was found split 21 to 14 across the hand tags on the IDENTICAL wording — and not one of the 21 tagged cards makes a real token, so the split was an inconsistency rather than a distinction, and the 2026-09-15 reading stands. Four more shapes were added the same day, all of them a body that never says "token" where a rule can read it. FOUR KEYWORDS: cloak and manifest dread turn a card face down as a 2/2, living weapon and job select hand the Equipment a Germ or a Hero to carry it; 29 reviewed cards carry one and 24 were already tagged. Plain MANIFEST is deliberately not among them, being one card and untagged. A TOKEN COPY counts only when what it copies is a CREATURE — Esoteric Duplicator copies an artifact and Firion an Equipment, and neither puts anything on the board to attack with — asked of the LINE that makes the copy, because the type word that answers it sits in the trigger beside the verb. A NAMED token whose creature type lives only in its reminder text ("Create a Spellgorger Weird token. (It's a {2}{R} 2/2 Weird creature…)") never says "creature token" anywhere. And the same sentence with the verb at the END, which no rule could read: Stridehangar Automaton says "those tokens plus an additional 1/1 Thopter … ARE CREATED instead". NARROWED FOR CREATURES 2026-09-24, the largest cut this tag has taken: a NONCREATURE card that makes a creature body is Tokens for a single one, while a CREATURE card is Tokens only when it makes THREE OR MORE at once or the effect REPEATS. The reason is how the cube is filed — a spell that makes bodies is counted among the creatures, and a creature already is one, so one Ally on the way in adds nothing it was not already counted for. 81 reviewed creatures lost the tag by the rule and 9 cards gained it; five more were slips found on the way (two printed token cards that make nothing, and a Treasure, a Clue and a proliferate). It also settled a split nobody had ruled: the identical Ally sentence was tagged on Katara and Invasion Reinforcements and not on Kyoshi Warriors or Treetop Freedom Fighters. Read per ability and WITH reminder text, where offspring, afterlife, fabricate, mobilize, myriad, squad and encore keep their bodies; a keyword is counted outside its reminder, which names it again (Dai Li Agents earthbends twice, not three times). X, "that many" and "for each" can always reach three and keep the tag, and so does a LIST ("a 2/2 Wolf, a 3/3 Beast and a 4/4 Beast"); a DELAYED trigger ("at the beginning of the next end step", Rukh Egg) and an ability that pays with the card itself (Leering Onlooker exiles itself from the graveyard) run once. The FRONT face is asked, so a Saga or Sidequest that transforms into a creature is still a noncreature card. A NONCREATURE permanent turned into a 0/0 creature and sized with counters is a body made, the same shape as earthbend, ruled 2026-09-25 on Case of the Filched Falcon ("put four +1/+1 counters on target noncreature artifact. It becomes a 0/0 Bird creature"). Two disagreements are left on purpose: Graywater's Fixer grants encore to every outlaw in the graveyard, which repeats but reads as one copy, and Siege-Gang Lieutenant repeats only while you control a commander, which a cube never has, and stays untagged by hand.
        Removal         = 1 << 1,   // answer ONE creature or planeswalker. A SHRINK counts, ruled 2026-09-15: a creature at zero toughness dies exactly as one that is destroyed, in both vocabularies — "target creature gets -3/-3" and "put a -1/-1 counter on target creature". Two edges matter. The toughness has to actually go down, so Icy-style "-2/-0" is Pacify, not this; and it has to be aimed at ONE named creature, because "each creature gets -1/-1" is a Wipe and is tagged as one. Widened 2026-09-17 so every rule here reads a QUALIFIED target — "damage to target ATTACKING creature", "target creature AN OPPONENT CONTROLS gets -X/-X" — and reads a fireball divided among several things, and an EDICT in any wording provided it is aimed at THEM ("each player sacrifices" costs you one too). Two shapes are deliberately out: a NONCREATURE target, which ends in the same letters and was reading as a kill, and an answer that can only touch what is already blocking the card, which is a combat trick. Widened again 2026-09-19, 113 wrong out of 5,037 reviewed cards down to 67. "EQUAL TO" is how the game writes one creature hitting another, and the rule read one word order, one length of filler and four destinations: the order reverses ("target creature deals damage TO ITSELF equal to its power"), the filler runs to 99 characters (Slash of Light), and the destination is qualified as freely as any other target ("to target creature AN OPPONENT CONTROLS", "to UP TO ONE OTHER target creature", "to EACH OF TWO OTHER target creatures", "to ANY OTHER target"). The AMOUNT too: "half X damage, rounded down" (Banshee), "X plus 1 damage" (Meteor Shower), "each of X targets" (Firestorm). An AURA is the third place the shrink lives and says "ENCHANTED CREATURE gets -2/-2" rather than "target". ANY toughness malus counts, ruled 2026-09-19: the size is not the question and neither is what happens to the power, so "+2/-1" (Funeral Charm), "+1/-1" (Coils of the Medusa) and "-0/-1" (Ironclaw Curse) are all this tag, and so is a "-2/-1 counter" (Contagion). A threshold tried first, requiring real power loss or two whole points of toughness, was reading four hand tags as a rule and they were slips. "-2/-0" stays Pacify, which is the same ruling from the other side: it is the TOUGHNESS that has to move. A shrink aimed at a creature YOU CONTROL is a pump with a price (Ashnod's Battle Gear). A creature on the BOTTOM of a library is answered (The Spot's Portal, Dramatic Accusation); on TOP it is handed straight back (Time Ebb). A FIGHT has to name what it fights: the bare word is also the name of a mode, and School Daze offers "Fight Crime", which counters a spell and draws a card. Three shapes match the words and answer nobody, and all three are read across the WHOLE card so that killing one of theirs and blinking one of yours keeps the tag: your OWN creature (Cold Storage, Safe Haven, Niko, Y'shtola), a creature CARD IN A GRAVEYARD (Eater of the Dead, Summoner's Sending), and a clause restricted to combat — which the game writes in the active voice ("target creature IT'S BLOCKING") and in the trigger ("WHENEVER THIS CREATURE BLOCKS") as often as the passive one the rule already knew. Measured and rejected, twice over: "destroy target <colour> permanent" as Removal. It ran a day on the reading that such a card is aimed at a creature in practice, and was ruled back to RemovePermanent on 2026-09-20 — the BREADTH is what that tag is for, the same reason Vindicate lives there. NB a "non\w+" alternation written to catch "nonblack permanent" also catches "NONLAND permanent", which is RemovePermanent's whole job — 26 false positives in one measurement.
        Counter         = 1 << 2,   // answer a spell on the stack by countering it
        RemovePermanent = 1 << 3,   // Vindicate / remove any permanent. A COLOUR-restricted kill is this tag too, ruled 2026-09-20: "destroy target red permanent" (Active Volcano, Flash Flood, both Paladins, the Elemental Blasts) answers whatever is in the way, and that breadth is the point even when you would usually point it at a creature. NB a "non\w+" alternation written to catch "nonblack permanent" also catches "NONLAND permanent", which the first rule already reads.
        Wipe            = 1 << 4,   // mass removal: the board is emptied, by any verb. Destroy, exile, damage, shrink, or return it all to hand — Desynchronization is a Wrath that happens to give the cards back, and "each player sacrifices all but one" is a Wrath somebody else performs. Widened 2026-09-15 to read past the ADJECTIVE, which is where half the sweepers in the game put one: Anarchy destroys all WHITE permanents, Perish all GREEN creatures, Evaporate damages each WHITE AND/OR BLUE one. Deliberately excluded: "destroy all creatures blocking or blocked by this creature" (Abu Ja'far, Kjeldoran Frostbeast, the Glyph cycle), which reads like a sweeper and is a combat trick.
        Bounce          = 1 << 5,   // unsummon / boomerang — a TARGETED return to hand (or a mass one). "Return this creature to its owner's hand" as an activation cost or an end-step drawback is a price the card pays, not an answer it offers. Narrowed 2026-09-15.
        Ramp            = 1 << 6,   // more mana, or sooner: a mana ability on a creature or rock, a land put onto the battlefield. A land tapping for its own single mana is just a land — with ONE exception, ruled 2026-09-15: a land that makes more mana than it costs EVERY turn (Ancient Tomb, Mishra's Workshop, the Karoo lands once they have given a land back). Mana that arrives without a {T} on a permanent you keep is also Ramp, ruled 2026-09-15: Black Lotus, Lotus Petal and Blood Pet spend THEMSELVES, and Dark Ritual is the same trade written as a spell. The one exception is a LAND THAT ENTERS TAPPED — Crystal Vein cracks for two and counts, Dwarven Ruins and Ebon Stronghold do the same thing and do not, because entering tapped costs the turn the extra mana was meant to buy. Eight more families ruled 2026-09-18, all of them mana you never had to make or a land you never had to draw: COST REDUCTION for other spells (the Medallions, the Crystals — but not "this spell costs {1} less", which was tagged 0 of 40); ANY activated mana ability on a non-land, whatever it charges (a tap for Llanowar Elves, a counter for Wall of Roots, a card out of hand for Elvish Spirit Guide, charge counters for the Mana Batteries); EXTRA LAND DROPS; MANA MULTIPLIERS (Mana Flare, High Tide, Wild Growth); a LAND OUT OF YOUR HAND onto the battlefield; UNTAPPING LANDS; a LAND ONTO THE BATTLEFIELD by any wording; and TREASURES, but only several at once or over and over — one Treasure once is a rider, the line already drawn for the Clue. Against all of that stands one subtraction, and it is the same ruling read backwards: MANA THAT COSTS MANA adds none, it converts colour, so Celestial Prism and Fire Sprites are ManaFixing and not Ramp — unless the trade comes back up, as Astrolabe pays {1} for two. Asked of every mana line on the card, so one free ability beside a paid one keeps the tag (Mana Prism). Two things the land family does NOT include, both learned by breaking them: the land handed to the player a removal spell was aimed at is their consolation, not your ramp (Emergency Eject, Price of Freedom), and ONE land sacrificed for ONE land is a swap (Renewal) while one for TWO is not (Harrow). The LANDER token is not in either exclusion and fetches a real land — all 17 reviewed cards that make one are Ramp.
        Disenchant      = 1 << 7,   // a targeted answer to an artifact or an enchantment, by any count and any verb: "destroy target artifact", "destroy UP TO ONE target artifact", "destroy X target artifacts", "exile TWO target artifacts", "destroy ANOTHER target artifact", and the mass form — Shatterstorm, Tranquility, Serenity, Seeds of Innocence. An EDICT aimed at an artifact answers one too, and unlike the creature edict a token cannot blank it: Pick Your Poison and Gaius van Baelsar are tagged here. AURA is the third noun, bare — Serene Heart and Hope Charm. Four shapes match the words and are not this tag. A SWEEPER that takes the creatures in the same breath is Wipe (Jokulhaups, Nevinyrral's Disk, Ultima, Death Begets Life) — every mass Disenchant that counts leaves creatures alone. An artifact CREATURE is a creature, so Chandler and Hearth Charm are Removal. An AURA ATTACHED TO a named thing is about that thing: Savaen Elves and Pyramids are land protection, Miracle Worker pulls an Aura off your own creature, Hakim and Gauntlets of Chaos strip the ones on a permanent they just touched. And your OWN is never an answer (Rats of Rath). The O-ring family splits on what it NAMES rather than on the exile coming back: "exile target ARTIFACT or creature … until this leaves the battlefield" counts (Mystical Tether, Detention Chariot, Trapped in the Screen), "exile target nonland PERMANENT" is RemovePermanent (Web Up, Seam Rip, White Auracite). Ruled 2026-09-19; took the tag from 43 wrong out of 5,037 reviewed cards to 2.
        Discard         = 1 << 8,   // strip cards from somebody else's HAND (Mind Rot, Hymn to Tourach, Liliana of the Veil). Like Mill, only outward: a bare "discard a card" is nearly always a cost — madness, blitz, cycling reminder text, the back half of looting — and paying it is not attacking anyone. Narrowed 2026-09-15.
        CardAdvantage   = 1 << 9,   // a card the opponent does not get: draw two or more, a draw you can go back to (an activated ability or a recurring trigger — an ETB fires once and is a cantrip), or "draw a card for each X", which is the same thing counted the other way round. PARITY is not advantage, and it wears three disguises: a loot draws and discards in one breath (that is Filter alone, ruled 2026-09-04), cycling pays a card to replace itself, and an ability that sacrifices its own permanent runs once. The CLUE and the IMPULSE are the borderline cases and both answer the same question: one card, once? A single Clue has to be cashed for {2} and a single impulse replaces the card that made it, so each is a rider — they earn the tag when the card makes them REPEATEDLY, and an impulse earns it outright when it takes more than one card off the top, which is a draw two wearing a different coat. Ruled 2026-09-11, parity carved out 2026-09-15, the one-for-one line drawn 2026-09-15. Parity is COUNTED, from 2026-09-16: a loot that draws three and discards one is a Careful Study with a bonus, so only a provable gain rescues the tag and anything unreadable stays parity. The same day closed four more ways the card is handed back without the word "discard" — Dream Cache puts it on top, Lat-Nam's Legacy shuffles it in, Jandor's Ring pays back the card just drawn. Three more shapes ruled 2026-09-16: the TOP OF YOUR LIBRARY is a second hand (Fblthp, Glarb, the Traveling Chocobo never run out even though they never draw); LOOK AT N AND TAKE MORE THAN ONE is a draw with selection, while taking exactly one is Filter; and CASTING OUT OF A GRAVEYARD OR EXILE counts only when you can go back to it every turn (Festival of Embers, Edgar), never for one flashback card. Ruled again 2026-09-19 and this time the count is symmetric: ANY net gain is CardAdvantage and stops being Filter, a net of zero is Filter and was never advantage. Four more ways a card arrives were measured and added the same day. WHOSE DRAW IS IT: a card that only ever draws for somebody else gives nothing away (Sibilant Spirit, Harbor Guardian, Phelddagrif) and 8 of those 10 cards are untagged, but "TARGET player" and "EACH player" are deliberately not in that list because you point Ancestral Recall and Braingeyser at yourself. TRIGGERING OFF a draw is not drawing (Underworld Dreams, Clinquant Skymage), 9 of 10 untagged. A draw that EXILES THE CARD ITSELF out of your graveyard runs once, the sibling of the one-shot sacrifice, and four of the fifty over-fires were identical Surveyors printed with that line. And the second-hand rule written on 2026-09-16 had a window too short for the very cards it named — Glarb puts 44 characters between "play lands" and "from the top of your library" where 40 were allowed, and Fblthp plots rather than plays — so it was widened to 15 cards of which 14 are tagged. Several Clues in one breath now count too, the reading the Treasure got the day before. THE CARD ITSELF IS A CARD, ruled later the same day, and it completes a count that had been half-done since 2026-09-16: Ponder is -1 for the Ponder and +1 for the draw, which is nothing, and Grab the Prize is -1 for the spell, -1 for the discard it charges and +2, which is also nothing and makes it Abandon Attachments with the discard moved into the cost line. So does every card the effect charges, including a discard paid as a COST and the lands Soldevi Sage feeds itself. A ONE-SHOT therefore has to draw two more than it pays before it has gained anything, while a REPEATABLE ability paid for the card once and never again and needs only one — which is the whole difference between Emmessi Tome and Racers' Scoreboard, two cards that print the same words on an activated ability and on an enters trigger. Repeatability and cost are read PER LINE, because they belong to the ability that loots: reading the colon in Uthros Scanship's Station text as its loot's activation cost made a one-shot look repeatable and handed it a card it never got. Four rulings on 2026-09-20 and they are one ruling read four ways. LOOK AT THE TOP FEW AND PUT ONE INTO YOUR HAND earns the tag when the ability REPEATS, which overturns the 2026-09-16 line that taking exactly one is Filter alone: Browse, Beastrider Vanguard and Water Tribe Rallier charge a cost you can pay again, while Morbius the Living Vampire prints the same words behind "Exile this card from your graveyard" and Lupinflower Village behind "Sacrifice this land", and those run once. The same question settles the IMPULSE — Elkin Bottle, Bruse Tarl and Cait Sith repeat, Equilibrium Adept's enters trigger does not — and it settles a repeatable draw whose COST IS A CARD OR A PERMANENT, which is advantage even so, because the ability bought that card once: Ahriman was the only untagged card among the twelve written that way. And a loot that only breaks even is Filter and nothing else however the halves are worded, which is why Brainstorm is not here (draw three, put two back) and neither are Rook Turret and Oblivious Bookworm ("you may draw a card. If you do, discard a card"), while Tersa Lightshatter earns it on a different line entirely — an attack trigger that exiles a card from your graveyard and lets you play it. Repeatability is now read PER ABILITY rather than per card, and that is what makes those splits possible: a modal bullet belongs to the trigger that introduced it, because Parapet Thrasher prints "Whenever one or more Dragons you control deal combat damage" three lines above its impulse, and a Saga chapter that names two numbers fires twice (Rediscover the Way's "I, II —"). Two of those were put to the human and RULED later the same day. EXILE SEVERAL AND PLAY ONLY ONE is Filter and not this: you saw two and played one, which is a count of zero — Riverwheel Sweep, Heroes' Hangout and Case of the Burning Masks all say it in the same words, and the last prints it behind "Sacrifice this Case" so it cannot repeat either. A REPEATABLE LOOT THAT LETS YOU PAY A PERMANENT INSTEAD of the card is Filter too (Chandra, Spark Hunter and Reckless Detective) — the ability is repeatable, but each use still trades one thing for one card, and that is selection. Three one-offs were recovered rather than left as known misses, because a ruling that does not reach the odd card has not been applied: Three Wishes puts 108 characters between exiling three cards and letting you play them where the rule allowed 80; Memories Returning reaches into your hand three times, one card at a time, and is advantage by the COUNT and not by repetition; and Mnemonic Sliver's "{2}, Sacrifice this permanent: Draw a card" is printed INSIDE QUOTES and handed to every Sliver, so the body that pays is a different one each time and the one-shot veto does not apply, the same reading OnlyPumpsInsideQuotes gives Buff. The LOOT guard is asked of the card with somebody else's discard blanked out, ruled 2026-09-20: parity means ONE player trading, and "each opponent discards a card AND YOU DRAW a card" is not a loot at all — it is their Discard beside your draw, which is what Jecht, Reluctant Guardian is hand-tagged. A SAGA CHAPTER THAT NAMES TWO NUMBERS fires twice, so the card it draws was bought once and drawn again; same reading ChapterFiresTwice gives the put-into-hand rule. Six more rulings on 2026-09-21. A REPLACEMENT draw — "the next time you would draw a card this turn, INSTEAD …" — adds nothing at all, it spends the draw you were going to have: all three reviewed cards are untagged and each is tagged for what it swaps the draw for (Aladdin's Lamp Filter, Mangara's Tome and Ring of Ma'rûf Tutor). A DELAYED cantrip ("draw a card at the beginning of the next turn's upkeep") is still a cantrip: 2 of the 48 reviewed cards that say it were tagged and both are slips. CYCLING is parity and always was: 2 of 45, same answer. A card that gives ITSELF A SECOND CAST is not one card, so the loot that nets zero on one cast comes out ahead over both — Welcome the Dead is -3 +4 with its flashback, Winternight Stories the same behind harmonize, Whispers of the Muse behind buyback. NB this is the OPPOSITE of what those same keywords mean for Regrowth, deliberately: there a card rebuying itself fetched nothing, here it really is a second helping of the draw. And a REPEATABLE DRAW WHOSE TRIGGER YOU CANNOT COUNT ON is not advantage. The human's words were "il punto è proprio la difficoltà di trigger"; there is no wording that means "this rarely happens", but the four cards each NAME their own difficulty and none has a counterexample — a coin flip (Goblin Artisans), becoming the target of an AURA SPELL or an ACTIVATED ABILITY, which needs a second card before the draw happens at all (Fugitive Druid, Professor Hojo), and a draw bought by handing an opponent a permanent (Stiltzkin). The line it does NOT cross is Surrak, Elusive Hunter: his trigger is ANY spell or ability an opponent aims at your creatures, which happens by itself, and he keeps the tag. Recorded as four cards read one at a time rather than a principle. Four wordings were simply unreadable and are now read: an ADDITIONAL card (Howling Mine, Sylvan Library), "draws UP TO n cards" without the "may" that separates Diminishing Returns from Truce, a trigger with one sentence in front of it (Rowen), and "that many cards FROM THE TOP of your library" (Symbiote Spider-Man, Choco, Stargaze). EXILE AND PLAY IT FROM THERE was confirmed as this tag on 2026-09-21 and was the single biggest hole left: twenty reviewed cards, every one tagged by hand, every one missed, because each writes the same idea differently. Five wordings now read — dig until a nonland card turns up and play it, which is what DISCOVER spells out (Territorial Bruntar, Solstice Revelations, Breaching Dragonstorm); a standing permission over a growing pile ("you may play cards exiled with this creature" — Evendo Brushrazer, Urianger, Triple Triad); that pile going to your HAND instead (Necropotence, Duplicity, Knowledge Vault); casting for free from among the exiled cards (Kefka, Portent of Calamity); and the top of SOMEBODY ELSE'S library (Vaan, Outrageous Robbery). The one line inside it: when the pile is THEIRS ALREADY the card is Steal and not this — Azula exiles a creature an opponent controls and a card from their graveyard, Crabomination does it in three zones, and both are hand-tagged without CardAdvantage, while Vaan and Outrageous Robbery take cards nobody had yet. Esoteric Duplicator was put to the human as the one card standing against the self-sacrifice rule and RULED to keep the tag: the trigger above its draw turns the sacrifice into a token copy of the same artifact, so the ability is on the table again and the card was never really paid. The tag was worked a SECOND TIME on 2026-09-21 and closed at 20 wrong out of 381 (precision 97.6%, recall 97.1%), and almost all of it was reading rather than ruling. Seven wordings joined the ones already read. The COUNT IS OPTIONAL in "look at the top few and put one into your hand": a single card off the top, every upkeep, is the same second hand the plural is (Darkstar Augur, Traveling Botanist, Bison Whistle, and Phyrexian Portal, which digs ten and says "LOOKS at" because an opponent does the looking) — 13 reviewed cards, 11 tagged. The same hand reached with a SEARCH counts when the ability repeats, which is exactly what separates Land Tax from Gift of Estates. A card SACRIFICED FOR MORE THAN ONE CARD is a trade it wins (Blitzball, Qiqirn Merchant, Reverberating Summons), which corrects the one-shot-sacrifice rule rather than overturning it — that rule was written for the one-card case and reads Preferred Selection right. Two exclusions were measured, not assumed: the count has to be READABLE (All-Fates Scroll draws "X cards, where X is the number of differently named lands" and is Ramp alone, the same answer an unreadable loot gets) and the line must not hand a card back (Conch Horn draws two and puts one from your hand on top, which with the artifact is level). EMPTYING YOUR HAND and drawing a fixed number back is not a loot, because the hand you paid can be empty — Case of the Crimson Pulse solves itself on "You have no cards in hand" and then draws two for nothing every upkeep; 3 cards, all 3 tagged. A TRIGGERED DRAW PRINTED INSIDE QUOTES has been handed to a body that keeps it (Thief's Knife writes it onto the Hero it makes, Herd Heirloom lends it for a turn), the same reading Mnemonic Sliver got — 5 cards, 4 tagged, and it must not be a loot, because Ninja's Blades hands out "draw a card, then discard a card". THE MONARCHY is a card at the end of every turn with no draw written down anywhere, and it outlives the permanent that granted it, so it is asked after the guards (Coin of Fate sacrifices itself to hand the crown over). ONE TRIGGER WORD COVERING TWO EVENTS is two cards: "when this artifact enters OR LEAVES the battlefield, draw a card" (Cryogen Relic). And three more were simply the same sentence backwards — "for each vote you received, draw a card" (Mob Verdict, Tempt with Bunnies), granting FLASHBACK to a whole graveyard rather than naming a cast (Iroh), and looking at the top and casting it after rather than before (Planetarium of Wan Shi Tong). The SAGA CHAPTER rule now reads any number of numbers ("I, II, III — Pain — You draw a card") and requires the draw to be UNCONDITIONAL, because a chapter that draws only when something else went its way is the conditional rider this tag has no ruling on: The Tale of Tamiyo draws when two milled cards happen to share a type, Vault 11 only when a vote produced no creature, and neither is tagged. Two boundaries were drawn. THE PILE IS THEIRS reaches their GRAVEYARD as well as their hand — six reviewed cards exile from a named opponent's graveyard and not one is CardAdvantage (Hama, Ashen Powder, Fire Nation Salvagers), because the card was already somebody's. And PARITY ACROSS THE TABLE is parity: "you draw a card AND target opponent may draw a card" (Tataru Taru), "you may have target opponent draw three cards. If you do, draw three" (The Legend of Yangchen), a loot every player may take (Mog) — three cards, none tagged, and the carve-out for "TARGET player" and "EACH player" still holds. Four guards were asked of the wrong thing and are now asked of the right one. The SELF-SACRIFICE guard must be the line that DRAWS (Cryogen Relic sacrifices itself to stun a creature on a different ability and was losing the tag to it). The EXILE-FROM-GRAVEYARD guard must ignore REMINDER TEXT, since encore and flashback both spell their cost as "Exile this card from your graveyard:" inside brackets (Eumidian Wastewaker). "Draw that many cards" is added back after the guards, so it has to ask the one that reads whose misfortune it counts (Kain hands himself to the player he hit; Mister Negative draws only if the life he swapped left him worse off). And the self-cost pattern had never fired at all: its "\b" had been written into the source as a literal BACKSPACE character by an earlier tool, invisible in every diff since. Four hypotheses were MEASURED AND REJECTED and are recorded so they are not re-derived. "A Saga chapter that draws is advantage" splits 7 to 13. "A modal bullet that draws under a trigger" buys Teval's Judgment and costs Monument to Endurance and Zuko, Conflicted — 1 to 2. "A card paid for with ANOTHER permanent is not free" is backwards: 9 of the 10 reviewed cards that sacrifice something else to draw ARE tagged, and Susur Secundi is the lone exception, left as a known miss rather than written into a rule. And "you may cast it from the top" must say LOOK or REVEAL, never EXILE, because "exile the top card, you may play it" is the one-card impulse this tag already calls a rider (Equilibrium Adept, Haste Magic, Summon: Brynhildr). AN ANTE CARD carries no tags at all, ruled by measurement on 2026-09-21: every one of them opens by telling you to take it out of the deck, so none is ever cast and none of their effects ever happens — nine are reviewed and all nine are untagged. That is a veto on the WHOLE classifier and not on this tag, and it lives at the top of Classify. The tag was CLOSED on 2026-09-22 at 2 wrong out of 380 — precision 99.7%, recall 99.7% — and the twelve questions that got it there were answered by the human in one sitting. Four answers were the tag already being right and the HAND TAG being realigned. The CONDITIONAL DRAW is not a principle at all: Haliya, Guided by Light and Stormbeacon Blade earn the tag because they REPEAT (an end step, an attack), not because of the "if", and Beza and Summon: Fenrir were the human's own slips and lost it. A DISCARD CHARGED AS AN ADDITIONAL COST is parity once the spell itself is counted — that is Grab the Prize's reading from 2026-09-19, and Laughing Mad and Sazacap's Brew now follow it and are Filter alone. "THEN DISCARD A CARD UNLESS …" is decided by the COUNT and never by the condition: Waterbending Lesson is -1 +3 -1 and comes out ahead even when the waterbend goes unpaid, Winternight Stories is the same behind harmonize, while Chakra Meditation and Katara draw one and hand one back and are Filter. Four more were the HAND TAG being right and the reading extended to say so. CASTING FROM THE TOP OF YOUR LIBRARY is the second hand whatever you pay for the card — Madame Web, Clairvoyant had been left untagged on a reading of "Spider spells" alone and is this tag. EMPTYING YOUR HAND TO REDRAW is advantage (Smellerbee, Rebel Fighter). SACRIFICING ANOTHER PERMANENT to draw is advantage, which closes the 9-of-10 measurement of 2026-09-21 at 10 of 10 (Susur Secundi). And a REPEATABLE INVESTIGATE is advantage, which takes that rule to 15 of 15 (Sophia, Dogged Detective) — the one card it was recorded as costing was not a cost. A LOOT SPREAD OVER TWO TRIGGERS is Filter and not this, ruled the same day: Teferi's Imp discards when it phases out and draws when it phases back in, which is one card for one card on a timer. Five more wordings joined the tag, each measured: CUMULATIVE UPKEEP PAID IN CARDS, which draws one more every turn the permanent survives and is written where a cost goes so nothing was looking there (Psychic Vortex); BOTH PLAYERS revealing the top and BOTH taking it, because symmetry is not parity when everybody gains — Howling Mine's reading, given by name (Parker Luck); SEVERAL TOKENS THAT EACH DRAW, which is the Clue count in a costume the Clue rules could not read and which the human put as "-1 +2 = +1" (Niko, Nyla); WARP as a second cast, so a card that pays you on the way OUT pays you twice (Anticausal Vestige) — deliberately not folded into the flashback family, which would reprieve 32 warp cards of which only 5 are tagged; and COPYING YOUR OWN SPELL, which is a second copy of a card paid for once (Taigam). The copy has to follow the trigger IMMEDIATELY, which is what separates Taigam from Mendicant Core, Guidelight, whose copy sits behind Max speed and a further {1} and which carries no tag. MAX SPEED is NOT a blanket difficulty: 34 reviewed cards print it and 4 are tagged. A third way an ability runs ONCE was added to the repeat gate: it TRANSFORMS the permanent the moment it succeeds, and the other face does something else. That is the whole difference between Sidequest: Catch a Fish and Traveling Botanist, which print the same sentence word for word — the Botanist looks every time it taps, the Sidequest turns into a land the first time it finds anything. Six reviewed cards transform themselves this way and five are untagged. And the trigger-difficulty family took two more, still read one at a time rather than as a principle: BENDING, a keyword action a deck has to be built to perform (Avatar Aang), and a CHOSEN CARD NAME, which asks you to name a card and then meet it that same turn (The Clone Saga, Demonic Consultation). TWO CARDS ARE LEFT and both are recorded rather than chased. Lich replaces lifegain with draws and the human called it "carta particolare, difficile da taggare"; it carries no tag at all and the classifier proposes this one. And Teval's Judgment offers a draw as a MODAL BULLET under a trigger, which was measured and rejected the day before: the shape buys that one card and costs Monument to Endurance and Zuko, Conflicted. Explained on 2026-09-22, and the three do not disagree — each falls to a rule this tag already has. Teval's Judgment says "hasn't been chosen THIS TURN", so the choice returns every turn and the second iteration is a card. Monument to Endurance triggers ON A DISCARD, so the draw is paid for with a card and is Filter. Zuko, Conflicted says "hasn't been chosen" with no reset, so the mode runs once in the game. The distinction is readable — "this turn" is there in the words — but the family was ruled too card-specific to encode, so no rule is written and Teval's Judgment stays a known miss.
        Filter          = 1 << 10,  // selection at NO net gain: you see more than you had to and the card count comes out level. Two shapes, ruled 2026-09-19: LOOK AT N AND TAKE ONE (scry, surveil, look at the top three and keep one), and HAND BACK WHAT YOU DREW (discard two draw two, and every rummage — "you may discard a card. If you do, draw a card"). The second was the tag's biggest hole: the rummage is the plainest Filter shape printed and the classifier had never read a single one of the 14 cards that carry it, 12 of which were already tagged by hand. The boundary with CardAdvantage is COUNTED, but the two tags are NOT EXCLUSIVE, ruled 2026-09-19 after a first cut of the count got that wrong: a card that selects AND comes out ahead carries both, because it really did both — Casting of Bones draws three and keeps two, which is a card gained and a choice made. Nothing ever withdraws this tag. Measured before the count was ruled: of the 41 reviewed cards that net zero, 38 were tagged Filter and 3 advantage. Anything whose count cannot be parsed stays parity and therefore stays here. Two things that look like this and are NOT: a card that pays a card as a COST to do something else (cycling, madness, blitz) — the discard is the price, not the selection — and looking at an OPPONENT'S library, which is only 2 of 8 reviewed cards and was measured and rejected. SCRY AND SURVEIL ARE THIS TAG UNCONDITIONALLY, ruled 2026-09-19: it does not matter how small the number is or what else the card does, so a surveil 1 tacked onto a bounce spell counts. That was measured before it was ruled and the opposite theory — that a small one is a mere rider — distinguishes nothing, because Filter is tagged on 88 of the ~103 reviewed cards that scry or surveil whether or not the card does something else. Read across every printed form, which "scry \d" was not: 17 cards say "scry X", 6 say "surveil X", and a handful say "scries" or "surveils" because the subject is a player. The count is required so that "whenever you scry OR surveil" — a trigger that watches one happen — stays out, the same reading that keeps "whenever you draw a card" out of CardAdvantage. EMPOWER JACE N joins them, ruled 2026-09-19: the Jace planeswalker token it makes exists to "[-1]: Surveil 1" and "[-3]: Draw a card", so the keyword filters by itself. 35 cards print it and 31 carry reminder text the surveil rule already read; the rule is for the other four. The token is a PLANESWALKER, so it earns nothing from Tokens. Looking at the top few and taking exactly one is still this tag, and from 2026-09-20 it is ALSO CardAdvantage when the ability repeats — Browse selects and gains, so it carries both, exactly as a profitable loot does. Two shapes were ruled INTO this tag the same day and out of CardAdvantage: EXILE SEVERAL AND PLAY ONE OF THEM (Riverwheel Sweep, Heroes' Hangout, Case of the Burning Masks), which is the impulse with a Filter's count, and a repeatable loot that lets you SACRIFICE A PERMANENT INSTEAD of discarding (Chandra, Spark Hunter, Reckless Detective) — repeatable or not, one thing goes for one card. And three were ruled OUT, from the nine cards where the classifier said Filter and the hand did not. CLOAK AND MANIFEST DREAD turn what you picked FACE DOWN into a 2/2, so what the ability gave you is a body and not a choice: Curator Beastie and Hide in Plain Sight are Tokens alone. The keyword is what names them, not "onto the battlefield", because a card put into play AS ITSELF is still a choice made — Aang, Gilgamesh, Jet, United Battlefront and Web of Life and Destiny all do that and all five are tagged here. A LAND off the top is Ramp (Famished Worldsire, Ignis Scientia), the same rail that makes a land search Ramp rather than Tutor. AND A LOOT THAT IS NOT ONE PLAYER'S: "each opponent discards a card AND YOU DRAW a card" is two players doing two different things, so Jecht, Reluctant Guardian is Discard and CardAdvantage and no more. Both of the first two vetoes stand down when the SAME LINE also puts a card into your hand, because then the card really did select — Planar Genesis takes a land if there is one and a CARD if there is not. The carve-out CardAdvantage already makes for "TARGET player" and "EACH player" holds here too: you can point Forget at yourself and Flux loots everybody including you, and both keep the tag. HIDEAWAY is certified Filter, ruled 2026-09-20 on Clive's Hideaway: you look at four and exile one you may play later, which is a hand you wait for. A LOOT SPREAD OVER TWO TRIGGERS is this tag, ruled 2026-09-22: Teferi's Imp discards when it phases out and draws when it phases back in, and neither line can see the other, so it is asked of the whole card after everything that could have called it advantage. Two more cards were realigned INTO this tag the same day and out of CardAdvantage, on the count that was already ruled rather than on anything new — Laughing Mad and Sazacap's Brew each pay a discard as an ADDITIONAL COST and draw two, which with the spell itself is exactly level, the reading Grab the Prize has carried since 2026-09-19.
        Reanimate       = 1 << 11,  // a creature back from the dead and straight onto the BATTLEFIELD, cheating its cost. Three more routes ruled 2026-09-19, because the graveyard is not the only place one comes back from and the tag was missing a third of what it should hold. A card THIS CARD EXILED, put onto the battlefield under your control, is the same trick with a waiting room in the middle (Purgatory, Sothera, The Darkness Crystal, Ghost Vacuum) — but blinking a creature YOU CONTROL is not, because Cold Storage and Safe Haven exile your own and hand it back, which is a strange kind of protection and stays untagged. A TOKEN COPY of a creature card in a graveyard is not the card itself, but what lands on the table is the same thing (Cursecloth Wrappings' embalm, Ardyn, Coiling Rebirth). And a LAND out of the graveyard is Ramp, not this, which follows from the 2026-09-18 ruling: 7 reviewed cards put one back and only 1 carried this tag. Measured and REJECTED the same day: vetoing the card that claws ITSELF out — unearth, "{B}: Return this card from your graveyard to the battlefield" — reads like the self-pump Buff refuses, and 16 of the 21 cards written that way carry no recursion tag, but doing it costs 7 points of recall across this tag and Regrowth and would have taken Hammer of Bogardan. That split is a ruling nobody has made.
        Buff            = 1 << 12,   // raise POWER and/or TOUGHNESS — that is the whole test, ruled 2026-09-18. A granted KEYWORD is not Buff, however good it is: flying, deathtouch, lifelink, first strike, indestructible change what a creature does, not what its numbers are, and the hand-tagging says so 49 cards to 5 (Jump and Cloak of Feathers are the same sentence and only one was tagged). DOUBLE STRIKE is the single exception, because doubling the damage is doubling the power by another name. A pump sized by X or by a count is a pump too. Only when it lands on SOMETHING ELSE (Giant Growth, Glorious Anthem, Bonesplitter): a creature that pumps only itself has a stat line, not an effect, so "{2}{R}: This creature gets +3/+0" and "Uril gets +2/+2 for each Aura attached to it" stay untagged. A +1/+1 COUNTER on somebody else is the same effect in the other vocabulary and counts too, ruled 2026-09-15 — one counter on one creature, support, distribute, all of it; only counters aimed at an OPPONENT'S creatures stay out. That ruling went AGAINST the measurement: of the 161 reviewed cards putting a counter on somebody else only 46 were tagged, the two halves were indistinguishable by wording, and 113 reviewed entries were realigned by hand the same day. It is the largest single correction the ontology has taken. The other half of that ruling points the other way: a pump restricted to one creature TYPE is NOT Buff — "Minotaur creatures get +1/+0" promises a payoff a cube with no Minotaur deck cannot collect. A COLOUR is not a tribe (Crusade, Bad Moon keep it) and neither is a STATE (Castle's "untapped creatures you control"); a lord that also pumps something unrestricted keeps it too. SIZE AND PURPOSE, ruled 2026-09-24/25, and it OVERTURNS "one counter on one creature" from 2026-09-15 for one-shots. The human's rule: Buff only when repeated, permanent (not tribal), at instant speed, over an area, or +5/+5 and more — and "a single one must be bigger than +1/+1 or have more than one target", where SINGLE means ONE-SHOT, not one target: the human's own re-review of Buff that day kept Expanding Ooze and Sample Collector (one counter, one target, every attack) and Fae Flight (+1/+0, permanent). Three rulings then fixed what SMALL (+1/+1, +1/+0, one counter) is worth, each asking what the card is FOR. B1: a small one-shot at sorcery speed is never Buff — "1 segnalino o un +1/+1 a sorcery non fa niente", and most of them are creatures entering. B2: a small one-shot at INSTANT speed is Buff only when the pump IS the card — Gift of the Viper and Guided Strike, not Magic Damper or Lightfoot Technique, which are Protection with a rider; read as "carries no other effect tag", with Filter not counting, since a scry is the rider the human calls saturating (Storm Strike keeps it). B3: a small REPEATED pump on a CREATURE is not Buff either — "an extra of a creature you judge as a whole"; on a NONCREATURE the pump is the card and stays (Firebreathing, Innkeeper's Talent, Collector's Cage). And a BITE is Removal alone at any size (Felling Blow, Bite Down on Crime): the pump is how the removal is aimed. Stonewood Invocation, +5/+5 and shroud, is Buff and Protection. 105 reviewed tags came off (64 B3, 27 B1, 8 B2, 5 bites, and one self-pump found on the way) and were handed back to the human unreviewed, at their request, because the rule had only settled in the last 200–250 cards of the sitting that produced it. Only the pumps the size reading recognises are judged; a double strike grant, a base P/T or a doubling is left standing and keeps the tag on its own (Origin of Spider-Man's chapter III). A bullet that carries its own trigger is timed by it (Hollowmurk Siege's "Abzan — Whenever you attack"), and a cost that exiles the card from its graveyard runs once (Wither and Bloom). B3 was CONFIRMED for noncreatures the same day: the 30 reviewed noncreature cards whose only pump is small and repeated "fanno solo Buff" and keep it. Ruled by name as well: a STATE in front of a tribe is still a tribe (Gornog's "Attacking Warriors you control"), and counters that ARE a body — on the creatures the sentence just made (Valgavoth's Onslaught) or on a noncreature that becomes a 0/0 (Case of the Filched Falcon) — are Tokens, not Buff; "those creatures" that were TARGETED rather than made stay Buff (Biogenic Upgrade, Smile at Death). Hurska Sweet-Tooth, Soulshriek and Endurance Bobblehead are Buff, the last with Protection. The self-pump ruling of 2026-09-18 was also found missing three wordings and they are read now: "it gets" when the clause in front of it is about the card (Slimy Piper, "as long as this creature is equipped, it gets"), the card's own name ("Rashka gets"), and counters doubled on this creature (Mossborn Hydra). On the human's re-review of 2024 (2026-09-25) the TRIBE was ruled out in every vocabulary, not only "Goblins you control get": counters on each of a tribe (Camellia's Squirrels, Kastral's Birds), a counter the tribe enters with (Slinza's Beasts), and a target that must belong to it (Inside Source's "target Detective", Rockface Village's list of four types) — and TOKENS are a tribe by another name (Hildibrand's "creature tokens you control get +1/+1", Sandstorm Salvager's counter on each creature token), so a pump only tokens get is a Tokens payoff and not Buff. The 12 older reviewed tags on the wrong side (Krenko, Minwu, Caretaker's Talent, Ezekiel Sims…) were taken off and handed back. The same re-review put the tag back on the one repeated small pump on a creature that grows ANOTHER each time it connects (Prowler, Scurry of Squirrels — 2 of 2, against 61 B3 creatures confirmed without it), and found the reading blind to double strike beside a quoted token pump (Blacksmith's Talent), to the creature just CAST entering with counters (Communal Brewing — but not the one a reanimation brings back), to scavenge granted to a graveyard (Young Deathclaws), to a counter "on a creature you control" in a mode bought five times (Season of Gathering), and to a new base on a stolen creature, which is the theft's contour (Coerced to Kill). Still open: Honor, a sorcery that is only the counter, was put back to Buff against B1; Kozilek's "other colorless creatures" vs It That Heralds the End, the same words tagged both ways; Apothecary Stomper's two counters at entry, untagged against the size rule.
        Protection      = 1 << 13,   // keep something alive, for somebody other than the card itself. TWO vocabularies, ruled equal on 2026-09-15: the keyword shield GRANTED to something else (Mother of Runes, Mystic Veil), and DAMAGE PREVENTION aimed at something else (Circle of Protection, Healing Salve, Orim). They do the same job — you hold them up and something survives — and the hand-tagged cards split 47 to 44 between them. Both clear the same gate: deployable in RESPONSE (an instant, flash, or an activated ability), so a bogle born with hexproof and a static Bubble Matrix are out; "This spell can't be countered" is the same self-shield written for the stack. Three shapes of prevention are deliberately excluded: a shield the card puts on ITSELF, a fog (it names no recipient), and preventing what a creature DEALS, which neutralises it and is Pacify — Maze of Ith and Gaseous Form are tagged that way. Regeneration is NOT in: measured, "regenerate target" cost 12 wrong for 4 right, so Death Ward stays untagged. Decided 2026-09-05, widened 2026-09-15.
        Burn            = 1 << 14,   // direct damage at a FACE (Lightning Bolt, Fireblast, Price of Progress). Damage pointed at a creature is how the game kills creatures and is already Removal, so Explosive Shot is Removal alone; "any target" is both, because it can go either way. A sweeper is Burn only when it catches the players too — Inferno's "each creature and each player" is Wipe AND Burn, Pyroclasm is Wipe alone. Damage to YOURSELF (Ancient Tomb, City of Brass, Juzam Djinn) is a price the card charges, not an effect. Ruled 2026-09-15. Seven more rulings on 2026-09-20, taken one family at a time from the 109 cards where hand and classifier disagreed, and most of them were the classifier reading one wording out of five. DAMAGE DIVIDED among bare "TARGETS" is Burn because a face can be one of them (Fireball, Rolling Thunder, Pyrotechnics, Meteor Shower, Mogg Mob), while "divided among target CREATURES" never reaches a player and is not (Pyrokinesis, Fire Covenant, Forked Lightning and four more). Fiery Justice is the exception the human ruled ON PURPOSE: it deals five and hands the same opponent five life back, so the count is nothing. THE WORD ORDER RUNS BOTH WAYS — "deals damage TO that player EQUAL TO the number of artifacts they control" as readily as "deals damage equal to … to that player" — and only one of the two was being read. THAT PERMANENT'S CONTROLLER is how the old punishers name a face (Psychic Venom, Ankh of Mishra, Dingus Egg and Staff, Haunting Wind, Seizures, Artifact Possession, Stinging Licid), but the same words hanging off an ANSWER are a removal spell with a bonus and are not this tag: Detonate, Icequake, Cinder Cloud and Stench of Evil destroy first, Misthios's Fury and Wisecrack shoot the creature first, and Judgment Bolt elides the second verb entirely. LIFE LOSS COUNTS FROM TWO. One point is a rider: eleven reviewed cards drain exactly one and carry no tag, against two that do, and one of those two (Susurian Voidborn) is word for word Al Bhed Salvagers, which does not. X counts, because X is not one, and so does "one life FOR EACH" of something. "LOSES LIFE EQUAL TO" splits on whose life it is — four cards say YOU lose it and none is tagged (Reanimate, Lich, Teval, Darkstar Augur), seven say somebody else does and six are. THE UPKEEP TAX IS BURN however slowly it burns, ruled after the hand tags came out ten to nine on cards printing the same sentence (Karma against Ancient Runes, Power Surge against Storm World). And LIFE PAID to stop a card is life lost (Breathstealer's Crypt, Sirocco, Cleansing), again from two, unless it is charged for each of something. The one ruling that went the other way: WHAT A TOKEN DOES IS THE TOKEN'S. Eight reviewed cards create the 0/1 Wizard that pings on every noncreature spell and none of the eight carries Burn, so the card that makes a body earns Tokens and the body's damage is the body's — keyed on "THIS TOKEN" inside the quotes, so an ability granted to a creature you already control keeps the tag (Black Mage's Rod says "this creature"). Both of the leftovers were ruled the same evening. A CREATURE WITH HASTE THAT IS GONE AT THE END OF THE TURN it arrived is a burn spell with legs: it attacks once and then is not there any more, so what it really did was put its power on a face. That covers the ones that die (Ball Lightning, Spark Elemental, Blistering Firecat, Groundbreaker, Lightning Skelemental) and the ones that BOUNCE (Viashino Sandstalker, Archwing Dragon, Glitterfang), and it is asked of the card with QUOTED text blanked out, because Strago and Relm, Skirk Alarmist and Apprentice Necromancer hand that drawback to somebody else's creature. NB this is the one rule in the file the reviewed set cannot really test: Ball Lightning is tagged, Viashino Sandstalker was not and is realigned by the ruling, and the other thirteen cards written that way are unreviewed. And on a LAND-DESTROYER the damage is a second effect the human reads as worth counting, which settles the split this family had — Orcish Mine charges the controller two for the land it just destroyed and is tagged, Icequake and Stench of Evil do the same and were not. A land is therefore not among the permanents whose death excuses the charge; a creature, an artifact, a permanent and a spell still are. Inferno is not a classifier problem at all: the store entry is keyed to a TEXTLESS theme card of the same name, and four more entries have the same wrong key (Bounty Hunter, Drowned, Grave Robbers, Spider-Verse).
        Sacrifice       = 1 << 15,   // a sacrifice OUTLET for your own creatures (Ashnod's Altar, Goblin Bombardment, Viscera Seer) — the half of the combo that makes a stolen creature worth taking, so read it next to Steal. NOT an edict, which empties somebody else's board (that is Removal's business), and not "as an additional cost to cast this spell, sacrifice a creature", which is a price paid once rather than a place to put things. Creatures, artifacts and anything named as a permanent all count — Atog and Infernal Tribute are Ashnod's Altar with different fuel — but LANDS do not: "sacrifice a land" is Harrow paying for a fetch, never an engine. Ruled 2026-09-15.
        Steal           = 1 << 16,   // threaten / act of treason / control magic (temporary or permanent control theft). An EXCHANGE is a theft you paid for: Juxtapose, Legerdemain, Political Trickery, Trade the Helm, Gauntlets of Chaos. Word of Command takes the PLAYER. And a card taken out of somebody else's LIBRARY, HAND or GRAVEYARD and played is a theft of a card rather than of a permanent — Outrageous Robbery, Apple of Eden, Laughing Jasper Flint, Rev, Crabomination, Azula. Playing YOUR OWN exiled card is not: Triple Triad and Wheel of Potential say "cards you own" after a symmetric exile, and an extra land drop or a second cast for the card itself belong to Ramp and Regrowth. GIVING A PERMANENT AWAY is the mirror image of this tag and was 12 of its 14 false positives — Jinxed Idol, Rainbow Vale, Chaos Lord and Stiltzkin pay it as a price, Rohgahh and Emberwilde Djinn suffer it for missing an upkeep, Guardian Beast negates it. The subject sits immediately in front of the verb, because "untap target creature AN OPPONENT CONTROLS and gain control of it" (Ray of Command, Magus of the Unseen) puts the victim in front of a verb that is yours. Reanimating out of an OPPONENT'S graveyard is both Reanimate and this (Ashen Powder, Bone Dancer, Chorale of the Void, Fire Nation Salvagers), but ordinary reanimation names no victim — Reanimate, Hymn of Rebirth and Coffin Queen say "from A graveyard" and stay Reanimate alone. "THEY may play" counts, but only against a NAMED zone: Gonti exiles from "that OPPONENT'S library" and hands the card to the creature's controller, while Transforming Flourish has one player exile from "THEIR library" and then cast it, which is nobody robbing anybody. A MILL is a zone too, and the only way Locke could ever be found — "each player mills a card … you may cast a spell from among those cards" names no library at all. Moving somebody else's AURA onto a permanent of your choosing is theft of its job if not of its control (Enchantment Alteration, Crown of the Ages). Two shapes were ruled OUT the same day: a token copy of their creature is a body you made rather than a card you took (Echo Chamber, which is Tokens), and a card pulled at random out of "that graveyard" names no victim (Mysterious Stranger). Ruled 2026-09-19; 42 wrong out of 5,037 reviewed cards down to 2, both of them realignments still to apply.
        Tutor           = 1 << 17,   // search library for a card (Demonic Tutor, Green Sun's Zenith, creature fetch) — selection/consistency, not raw card advantage. Widened 2026-09-19: the game names what it fetches in every way there is, and the old rule knew only "a card" and five card TYPES. A SUBTYPE (Equipment, Demon, Vehicle, Dragon), a COLOUR ("a black card, a green card, and a blue card"), a COUNT ("three cards"), a NEGATION ("a nonland permanent card"), a second ZONE ("your library and/or graveyard"), a search somebody else makes of their OWN library (Noble Benefactor), and the sideboard, which the rules call "outside the game" (Ring of Ma'ruf). Two things are NOT this tag. A LAND search is Ramp or ManaFixing — the ruling the tag was written around, and it costs Elemental Teachings, one card against the family; a land on one side of an "or" does not make the whole search a land search (Starfield Shepherd). REVEALING UNTIL a type turns up hands you whichever one was nearest the top rather than one you chose — The Regalia, House Cartographer, Yuna's Whistle, Hei Bai and Sacred Guide are all untagged, while Demonic Consultation digs for a NAME you picked and counts. Naming the card you want was vetoed for half a day on the evidence that five reviewed cards said it and none was tagged; ruled the other way the same day, because it is the purest form of the tag whether it finds another copy of itself, a combo piece, or somebody else's payoff (Raven Clan War-Axe, Tower Winder), and the veto was costing Dragonstorm Forecaster. Searching without putting the card in your HAND counts too — Ugin exiles and casts them free, Goblin Recruiter stacks the top of the library, Foresight just exiles. 26 wrong out of 5,037 reviewed cards down to 1, and that one is Elemental Teachings, held out by the land ruling on purpose.
        ManaFixing      = 1 << 18,   // mana colour fixing (dual/fetch lands, multicolour rocks) — distinct from Ramp; a card can be both. Two shapes added 2026-09-15 that never say "or" on one line: TYPECYCLING, which fetches the colour you are short of, and a land with TWO mana abilities in different colours (Bleachbone Verge and the whole modern cycle). One shape taken out: mana you may ONLY SPEND on one thing is not fixing, it buys the card the designer had in mind — judged per line, so a card with one restricted ability and one free one still counts. A SEARCH FOR A LAND was measured and rejected once, then split by DESTINATION on 2026-09-18 and half of it came back: fetched to HAND it fixes the colour you are short of and ramps nothing, because the land still has to be played off your one land drop (tagged on 28 of 30 reviewed cards); put straight onto the BATTLEFIELD it is the extra mana rather than the colour, and stays Ramp (8 of 51). Asked undivided the family is 42% and reads like noise, which is what the first measurement saw. The other ruling of that day cuts the opposite way: mana you get for NOTHING BUT A TAP is a mana SOURCE, so the card is Ramp and the colour is a property of the source rather than a service — Birds of Paradise, Mox Jasper, Solar Array are Ramp alone (a bare {T} for any colour was tagged on 2 of 28). Pay something on top — mana, a life, the tap of another permanent — and it converts colour instead of making it, which IS the job: Celestial Prism, Mana Prism, Gene Pollinator (11 of 12). A LAND is exempt from that and never asked, because a land makes no mana you did not already have and the colour is the only thing it gives you (17 of 17). Two more added the same day: EVERY LAND FLAVOUR OF CYCLING, where "basic landcycling" alone is printed on 125 cards — more than all five named types together — while slivercycling and wizardcycling fetch a body and stay out; and the TREASURE, which is a Lotus Petal in token form and fixes however many you get, the count deciding only whether it also ramps. And the COLOUR FILTER, the mirror of the Ramp ruling: you feed it mana and it hands back a colour you did not have. Generic in and a colour out always counts (Farrelite Priest "{1}: Add {W}", Sea Scryer, Coal Golem); a colour in counts only when a DIFFERENT colour comes out (Fire Sprites "{G}, {T}: Add {R}", Agent of Stromgald), because Evendo pays green for more green and that is Ramp. Colourless output fixes nothing. Named in full: bare "Fixing" read as "fixing what?" (mana or hand), and the tagger shows this name verbatim. Old "Fixing" tags still parse via the alias table.
        Pacify          = 1 << 19,   // soft/pseudo removal: neutralise SOMEBODY ELSE'S creature without destroying it (tap-lock/Icy Manipulator, Pacifism/Arrest, detain), INCLUDING by preventing what it deals — Maze of Ith and Gaseous Form, the reading ruled on 2026-09-05 and finally implemented on 2026-09-15. Three things are not this tag, all of them the same question asked again: UNTAPPING a creature (the rules were reading "untap target creature" as "tap target creature", which was a quarter of the false positives), tapping a creature YOU control as a cost (Energy Tap), and a card that stops YOUR OWN creatures attacking (Akron Legionnaire). The prevention half asks the same question twice more: Mtenda Lion prevents what IT deals, which blunts its own attack, and Ebony Horse prevents what a creature YOU CONTROL deals, which is vigilance bought at instant speed — Foxfire says almost the same sentence and is Pacify, so the whole difference is "you control". And a lock the OPPONENT CAN BUY OUT OF is a tax rather than a lock — Heroism and Winter's Chill let the attacker pay and come through, so whether anything is neutralised is their decision (ruled 2026-09-16, "non c'è certezza"). Unconditional prevention IS the tag: Safeguard, Warning and Subdue say what Horn of Deafening says and were realigned to match. A card that only restrains ITSELF is a stat line, not an effect: "this creature can't attack" is defender, printed on a Wall, and "can't attack unless defending player controls an Island" is a drawback. Narrowed 2026-09-15, which was the single largest source of noise on the board. The tag was worked on 2026-09-22 and closed at 17 wrong out of 164 — precision 78.8% -> 95.1%, recall 62.1% -> 94.5% — and the finding was not a reading problem: THE HAND TAGS CONTRADICTED THEMSELVES on identical wording. Six pairs were put to the human and all six were resolved the same way. Twiddle against Twitch, Riptide ("tap all blue creatures") against Blinding Light ("tap all nonwhite creatures"), Word of Binding ("tap X target creatures") against Crashing Wave, Storm Elemental and Sunstar Chaplain against Sterling Keykeeper and Starport Security, Koskun Falls against Propaganda, and Ultimecia against them all. A PLAIN TAP IS THIS TAG, and both proposed lines were rejected: the lock need not LAST — "altrimenti saltano il punto e tutti i tappini", and Detain is temporary and has always been here — and tapping need not be the card's main job. 39 reviewed entries were realigned onto the tag that day, which is what the rest of this paragraph is measured against. Four boundaries hold it in. THE VICTIM MUST BE ABLE TO BE A CREATURE: "tap target artifact" is mana denial and neutralises nobody (Relic Barrier, Touchstone, Phyrexian Gremlins, and Hyperion Blacksmith, which twiddles an artifact where Twiddle itself names a creature). AN UNTAP LOCK ON LANDS is mana denial too, ruled "per le terre niente Pacify" — Choke and Curse of Marit Lage stop Islands and Winter's Night a snow land, and none is tagged; asked per LINE and phrased as "names a land and nothing else" rather than "fails to name a creature", because an Aura says "enchanted PERMANENT doesn't untap" and names neither (Flood the Engine, Tractor Beam, Stuck in Summoner's Sanctum). TAPPING ON YOUR OWN ATTACK TRIGGER is a combat trick and not a lock: it clears one blocker for a swing already happening, and seven reviewed cards do it with none tagged (Seasoned Marshal, Sidar Jabari, Conformer Shuriken, Thunder Lasso, Web-Shooters, Vengeful Villagers, Kimahri, whose "at the beginning of combat on your turn" is the same trigger from the other side). And "<this creature> CAN'T ATTACK OR BLOCK UNLESS …" is the price of a statline — the Wall reading of 2026-09-15, which could not see this wording because "can't attack or block" vouches for the sentence before anyone asks whose creature it is; read per line, and an older card writes its own name (Hazoret Godseeker, Ketramose, Sab-Sunen, Patchwork Beastie, Tiger-Dillo, The Lion-Turtle). Five wordings were added the same day. A STUN COUNTER is how every card printed since 2021 writes an untap lock, and it has to land on somebody else — Tonberry, Baloth Prime and Ambling Stormshell stun themselves as a drawback. The PLURAL "creatures DON'T untap" was missing entirely, and with it every classic lock (Meekstone, Marble Titan, Mudslide, Dream Tides, Magnetic Mountain, Thelon's Curse, An-Zerrin Ruins, Wrath of Marit Lage); so was "players skip their untap steps" (Stasis, Sands of Time). The Propaganda tax says "can't attack YOU unless", which the rule could not read because it wanted punctuation straight after the verb. NEUTRALISING WITHOUT KILLING is the tag's other half and was ruled in wholesale: base power 0 (Island of Wak-Wak, Singing Tree, Sorceress Queen), becoming a small NAMED body (Spider-Man No More's 1/1 Citizen with defender, Honest Work's Humble Merchant, Unable to Scream's 0/2 Toy — the [0-2] is what keeps Lizard, Connors's Curse and Titania's Song out), a shrink that also strips the abilities (Fresh Start), and "attacking creatures get -1/-0" (Weakstone). A BARE "-5/-0" is not here: measured and rejected at 2 tagged out of 12, and Cryoshatter is the one card that costs. PHASING OUT is this tag AND Protection, ruled together because the same words do both jobs — "puoi usarlo sia sull'opponent per prendere tempo che su di te per salvare qualcosa"; the permanent has to be a creature (Vision Charm phases an artifact) and an O-ring phase-out that lasts until the enchantment leaves belongs to the temporary-exile family, which still has no ruling (Oubliette, Tawnos's Coffin, Summon: Ixion, and Icy Prison, which the human called their own mistake and moved to Removal). And PREVENTING DAMAGE TO THE PLAYER ALONE is this tag and NOT Protection — "le prevenzioni al solo giocatore mettiamole come solo Pacify": nothing of yours is saved, the attack simply stops mattering, which is what a Propaganda does. A prevention that also covers permanents keeps both (Ultimate Magic: Holy hands your permanents indestructible on the same line), and so does one that answers a SOURCE (Prismatic Strands). Finally, REMINDER TEXT and worked EXAMPLES lock nobody down — Primal Clay explains defender with "(A creature with defender can't attack.)" and Balduvian Shaman quotes "black creatures can't attack" twice inside an example of text-changing — so the patterns are re-asked with the brackets stripped.
        LandDestruction = 1 << 20,   // destroy/exile lands (Stone Rain, Sinkhole, Wasteland, Armageddon). Deliberately narrow: attacking the mana base by removing lands, NOT the wider "mana denial" family (Winter Orb, Blood Moon, Sphere effects), which stays untagged. Distinct from RemovePermanent, whose rules only read the literal "destroy target permanent" of a Vindicate. Widened 2026-09-19, where all 23 of the misses were: the land is almost never alone in the sentence. It sits in a TYPE LIST ("destroy target artifact, creature, or land" — Aftershock, Creeping Mold), behind a COUNT ("destroy up to one target artifact, up to one target creature, and up to one target land" — Boom Box), beside a SECOND TARGET (Fumarole), or in a mass form that takes the creatures with it ("destroy all creatures and lands" — Devastation). A BASIC LAND TYPE is a land by another name and the only wording Boil, Acid Rain, Reign of Chaos and Volcanic Eruption ever use. Three more name the victim without saying "target land": Cleansing's "for each land, destroy that land", Desolation's land edict with the victim clause in between, and Planetary Annihilation's "chooses six lands they control, then sacrifices the rest". Fallow Earth answers a land without destroying it. Four shapes match the words and are not this: a land card already IN A GRAVEYARD is fuel (Steward of the Harvest), an AURA ATTACHED TO a land protects it (Savaen Elves, Pyramids), your OWN denies nobody anything (Rats of Rath), and a rebalance that destroys nothing and hands basics back is not denial (Natural Balance) — that last excuses only a line with no destroy or exile in it, because Sandworm, Price of Freedom and Magmatic Hellkite all kill a land and offer a basic as consolation. 23 wrong out of 5,037 reviewed cards down to 4.
        Mill            = 1 << 21,   // put cards from a library into a graveyard (Glimpse the Unthinkable, Millstone, Altar of Dementia). Only when it is aimed at SOMEBODY ELSE. A bare "mill three cards" fills your own graveyard, which is fuel for what the card does next rather than an attack on anybody — Stinkweed Imp's dredge and Fell Gravship's self-mill are graveyard cards, not mill cards. Narrowed 2026-09-15; the older cost exclusion (Deep Spawn, Millikin) follows from the same reading, since a price is always paid by yourself.
        Regrowth        = 1 << 22,  // a card from a graveyard back to HAND — the sibling of Reanimate, same origin, different destination. The TOP OF A LIBRARY counts too, ruled 2026-09-19: it is a hand you wait one turn for, and all 5 reviewed cards written that way were already tagged. The BOTTOM is not in it, because putting a card on the bottom of a library is graveyard hate (Barkform Harvester, Chrome Companion), and neither is an OPPONENT'S graveyard, which is Misinformation shuffling their answers back rather than rebuying your own. CASTING ANOTHER CARD out of a graveyard IS this, ruled later the same day: it is reanimation for spells — the card never reaches your hand, but it is bought back from the same place and for the same reason (Edgar, Festival of Embers, Noctis, Iroh handing every card in the yard flashback). The card that gives ITSELF a second cast is NOT, and that split is what rescued a family which first measured at 10% and looked like noise: read apart, flashback and its cousins are 54 reviewed cards of which exactly ONE carries this tag — Sorceress's Schemes, which earns it on a different line by returning a card to hand — while the aimed version is 12 cards of which 5 were already tagged. Three shapes read like a cast and are not: cost reduction COUNTING cards in the yard ("costs {1} less for each creature card in your graveyard"), a card exiled from there as an additional COST (Haunting Misery), and a trigger that only WATCHES a cast happen (Neerdiv), the same shape that keeps "whenever you draw a card" out of CardAdvantage.   // return a card from a graveyard to HAND (Regrowth, Raise Dead, Eternal Witness). Deliberately NOT merged with Reanimate, which puts it straight onto the battlefield: one hands back a card you still have to pay for, the other cheats the cost — different speeds, different decks.
        Redirect        = 1 << 23,   // act on a spell already on the stack WITHOUT countering it: change its target (Misdirection, Deflection) or copy it (Fork, Reverberate, Twincast). Widened 2026-09-19 for the possessive: Meddle says "change THAT SPELL'S TARGET to another creature" where Deflection says "change the target of", and no rule reading the second could see the first. The DELAYED COPY was tried the same day and ruled OUT: "when you next cast an instant or sorcery spell this turn, copy it … you may choose new targets for the copy" doubles a spell of YOUR OWN rather than acting on somebody else's on the stack. It matched 8 reviewed cards, 4 tagged and 4 not, and Ether reads word for word like Jeong Jeong — the ruling took the tag off all eight rather than keep a rule that could not tell them apart. Storm's reminder text says the same words and was never this either. What stays is the AIMED copy, "copy TARGET instant or sorcery spell": Fork, Reverberate, Twincast. Kept as one tag because both do the same job in a deck — they turn an opponent's spell into your problem-solver — and because splitting them would give two tags of a few dozen cards each. Distinct from Counter, which answers by removing the spell.
        Cheat           = 1 << 24,   // put a NONLAND permanent onto the battlefield without casting it — out of your hand (Sneak Attack, Elvish Piper, Quicksilver Amulet, Didgeridoo, Show and Tell) or out of your library (Natural Order) — or hold a STANDING permission to cast without paying (Dracogenesis, Aluren, Omniscience). Ruled 2026-09-18, and it is the tag those cards had been falling through: of the 13 already reviewed that put a card from hand onto the battlefield, 7 carried no tag at all. Three edges. The GRAVEYARD is not in it — that is Reanimate, which keeps its own tag: same trick from a different zone, but 97 hand-tagged cards and an archetype of its own, and folding them together would cost all 97 to buy nothing. A LAND is not in it — putting a land onto the battlefield is Ramp, ruled the same day, so Skyshroud Ranger and PuPu UFO stay there. And "you may cast IT without paying its mana cost" on one exiled card is the impulse rider, which is CardAdvantage: only a standing permission counts, the same line already drawn for the Clue and the Treasure. Measured before it was added: the hand-to-battlefield wording is on 119 cards in the bulk, while the bare phrase "without paying its mana cost" is on 624 and is almost all that rider.
    }
}
